 left so don't allow them to  change the narrative we're against  illegal voters they barely exist though  and the real issue is voter suppression  it's not voter fraud and this has been a  at this point I don't know it is could  we say it's a multi-decade effort to  suppress votes in a way that is  strategically advantageous to  Republicans don't let them play the  games a good that this guy Whitley has  resigned bad that he was able to  whitewash the resignation with this  bogus resignation letter and now we've  got to see who it is that the governor  Abbott is going to nominate probably  won't be out until after the 2020  election we have a voicemail number  number is two one nine two David P take  a listen to this voicemail that came in  about social democracy and technological  unemployment  hey David my name is Justin I'm from  Washington State my question is as a  social democrat should we be opposed to  automation taking service jobs away from  working-class people thank you okay so  this is a really good question whether  you're a social democrat or not the the  right answer here is a simple one  historical efforts to oppose technology  on the basis that it would destroy jobs  have failed period we could probably  find some examples where there has been  a successful effort to suppress a  technology at least temporarily on the  basis that it would destroy jobs but  historically saying we need to suppress  this coming technology because people  will lose their jobs has not worked what  we need to take seriously is rethinking  the relationship of humans to work for  example technological automation if you  imagine a business with a hundred  employees technological automation could  make 50 of those employees unnecessary  and one way that the business could  respond would be to say we're replacing  50 employees with machines we're keeping  50 employees 50 people lose their jobs  it doesn't have to go that way the other  way that it could be done is the  business says well what we're going to  do is keep all of our hundred employees  but they'll each work only two and a  half days a week or four hours a day  instead of eight or whatever else now  obviously the business is not going to  be willing to use the entire gain from  the technology and and give it all to  the workers but maybe they say we'll  keep our hundred employees and they can  work six or five hours a day instead of  eight and we will sort of split the  gains from the technology now obviously  there needs to be some reason that the  business would choose to do that and  there are people including Bill Gates  who have started floating ideas and and  sort of thinking about different ways in  which we might be able to make that  happen but no I think that it's not only  misguided I think most importantly it's  a futile effort to try to oppose the  technology on the basis that jobs will  be lost we need to rethink how the gains  from these technologies should be  distributed and that's  where 90% of the issue is okay I will  tell a bunch of vacation stories on the  bonus show today including to dog poop  stories I don't know that we've ever  really had dog poop stories on the show  and I regularly visit Argentina so it  makes it all the more incredible that  there haven't been dog poop stories on  the show anybody who's walked around  when a cytus will understand what I mean  but I do have two dog poop stories today  and a whole bunch of other stuff become  a member or a patron to get instant  access to the bonus check  the david pakman show at david  pakman.com  [Music]
 

